:sym typeface="Calibri"/></a:rPr><a:t>+</a:t></a:r><a:r><a:rPr b="0" i="0" lang="en-US" sz="2800" u="none"><a:solidFill><a:schemeClr val="dk1"/></a:solidFill><a:latin typeface="Calibri"/><a:ea typeface="Calibri"/><a:cs typeface="Calibri"/><a:sym typeface="Calibri"/></a:rPr><a:t>-glucose symporter and a glucose uniporter operate on opposite sides of epithelial cells to facilitate movement of glucose from the intestine to the blood.</a:t></a:r><a:endParaRPr/></a:p><a:p><a:pPr indent="-165100" lvl="0" marL="342900" marR="0" rtl="0" algn="l"><a:spcBef><a:spcPts val="560"/></a:spcBef><a:spcAft><a:spcPts val="0"/></a:spcAft><a:buClr><a:schemeClr val="dk1"/></a:buClr><a:buSzPts val="2800"/><a:buFont typeface="Calibri"/><a:buNone/></a:pPr><a:r><a:t></a:t></a:r><a:endParaRPr b="0" i="0" sz="2800" u="none"><a:solidFill><a:schemeClr val="dk1"/></a:solidFill><a:latin typeface="Calibri"/><a:ea typeface="Calibri"/><a:cs typeface="Calibri"/><a:sym typeface="Calibri"/></a:endParaRPr></a:p></p:txBody></p:sp><p:pic><p:nvPicPr><p:cNvPr descr="An illustration of glucose transport in intestinal epithelial cells.&#xA;With a pink background, identified as intestinal lumen, this has a yellow representing the epithelial cell. The image has flat sides on the right, and the left side has several waves going in and out. These are consider the apical surfaces and are identified as microvilli. The right edge of the middle image is the basal surface. On one of the microvilli on the left is a Na+-glucose symporter (driven by high extracellular [Na+[). An arrow beginning with 2Na+ (2 pink circles) and another beginning with glucose (blue hexagon) go through the protein and point to their counterparts in the middle of the cell. In the middle of the cell are the two pink circles, which have a dashed line pointing to an arrow. Above these is one pink circle with a dashed line pointing to that same arrow. This arrow goes through Na+K+ ATPase on the outside edge and points to 3Na+. From the outside 2K+ (with two green balls) has an arrow going through the Na+K+ ATPase and pointing to two green circles. A glucose uniporter GLUT2 (facilitates downhill efflux) is on the edge of the cell. The green hexagon glucose has an arrow pointing through the glucose uniporter to glucose on the outside." id="578" name="Google Shape;578;p78"/><p:cNvPicPr preferRelativeResize="0"/><p:nvPr/></p:nvPicPr><p:blipFill rotWithShape="1"><a:blip r:embed="rId3"><a:alphaModFix/></a:blip><a:srcRect b="0" l="0" r="0" t="0"/><a:stretch/></p:blipFill><p:spPr><a:xfrm><a:off x="2514600" y="2819400"/><a:ext cx="4324350" cy="3883025"/></a:xfrm><a:prstGeom prst="rect"><a:avLst/></a:prstGeom><a:noFill/><a:ln><a:noFill/></a:ln></p:spPr></p:pic></p:spTree></p:cSld><p:clrMapOvr><a:masterClrMapping/></p:clrMapOvr></p:sld>